 "limitations": [
  {
   "wanted": "A positive mechanical snap (flexible fingers or a thin retainer collar) that physically hooks over the top of the M3 nut inside the knob's mouth.",
   "blocked_by": "Printability of a single self-supporting PLA piece: the nut's corner-to-corner span (6.35 mm) exceeds its across-flats (5.5 mm), so any rigid or thick-backed snap ring at the mouth would have to stretch ~0.7 mm radially to let the nut through. Fingers thin enough to flex that far can only bend tangentially, but a mouth-up print turns every downward-facing barb into a bridged roof, and the nut's corner tips loaded the trap mostly radially (stiff) instead of tangentially (compliant) \u2014 a 4-corner/3-barb geometry mismatch as well.",
   "why": "It would make nut retention independent of print tolerance and of PLA creep. Instead the nut is retained by a tapered, deliberately undersized hex pocket (a press fit that self-selects its own seating depth) plus a hard mechanical stop: the 3.4 mm hole in the closed end, which the nut can never be pushed through. A printed coupon with four gauge pockets is included so the fit can be confirmed before the knob is relied on."
  }
 ],
